description:
[narrowed from route-edit-cd20dbbc] Stop the gap store from silently destroying itself in repos/development-vessel/src/resolvers/substrate-gap.ts.

Measured incident, 2026-09-09: the gap store went from 4,113 rows to 44 in a single day. Cause: loadGaps() returns an EMPTY array on ANY read or parse failure, and the store is a read-modify-write - load, mutate, save. So one corrupt or transiently unreadable gaps.json yields [], and the very next save renames that near-empty array over the good file, destroying every gap. A corrupt leftover temp from 11:18 (invalid control character mid-file) is consistent with exactly this. Recovery required an operator merging a 9MB orphaned snapshot back in by hand.

The distinction that matters: a MISSING file legitimately means an empty store; an UNREADABLE or UNPARSEABLE file means the data is still there and must not be overwritten.

Find this line, which occurs exactly once in the file:

    return JSON.parse(raw) as SubstrateGap[];

Replace it with exactly these six lines:

    const parsed = JSON.parse(raw) as SubstrateGap[];
    if (!Array.isArray(parsed)) throw new Error("gaps.json did not parse to an array - refusing to treat as empty");
    return parsed;
  } catch (err) {
    if ((err as NodeJS.ErrnoException)?.code === "ENOENT") return [];
    throw err;

Note carefully: the replacement deliberately opens a new catch clause, because the existing catch line and the return [] line immediately below become the body of that new clause. After the edit the function must read: try, read file, parse, guard that it is an array, return it; catch err, return [] only when the error code is ENOENT, otherwise rethrow.

Effect: a missing file still yields an empty store, which is correct on first boot. A corrupt or unreadable file now throws, so the caller aborts instead of persisting an empty array over real data. Losing a single write is recoverable; losing the store is not.

Change nothing else in the file and add no imports.

